1. Difference Between ALLOW_ANY and REGISTRY_ONLY

These are Istio outbound traffic modes configured via Sidecar.

ALLOW_ANY (Default)
YAML
outboundTrafficPolicy:
mode: ALLOW_ANY

Behavior:

Plain Text
Pod → google.com ✅
Pod → github.com ✅
Pod → anysite.com ✅

Istio forwards traffic even if it has never heard of the destination.

Flow:

Plain Text
Pod
|
Envoy
|
Internet
REGISTRY_ONLY
YAML
outboundTrafficPolicy:
mode: REGISTRY_ONLY

Behavior:

Plain Text
Pod → google.com ❌
Pod → github.com ❌
Pod → unknown.com ❌

Only destinations registered in Istio are allowed.

Registered means:

Kubernetes Service
ServiceEntry

Flow:

Plain Text
Pod
|
Envoy
|
Known Service ?
|
+-- YES --> Send
|
+-- NO --> Block
Interview Answer

ALLOW_ANY permits outbound traffic to any external destination. REGISTRY_ONLY blocks traffic to unknown destinations and allows only services registered in the mesh through Kubernetes Services or ServiceEntries.

2. Why Does Istio Need ServiceEntry for External Services?

Istio maintains a service registry.

Normally it knows:

Plain Text
app1.demo.svc.cluster.local
app2.demo.svc.cluster.local
 

But it doesn't know:

Plain Text
google.com
api.github.com
amazon.com

When REGISTRY_ONLY is enabled:

Plain Text
Unknown destination = blocked

ServiceEntry teaches Istio about external services.

Example:

YAML
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: ServiceEntry
metadata:
name: google
spec:
hosts:
- google.com

Without ServiceEntry:

Plain Text
curl google.com
↓
Blocked

With ServiceEntry:

Plain Text
curl google.com
↓
Allowed
Interview Answer

ServiceEntry extends Istio's service registry to include external services. Without it, Istio cannot apply routing, security, or observability policies to external destinations.

3. NetworkPolicy vs ServiceEntry

A very common interview question.

NetworkPolicy

Controls:

Plain Text
Who can talk to whom?

Example:

Plain Text
app1 → app2 ✅
app1 → app3 ❌

Works at:

Plain Text
Layer 3 / Layer 4
IP + Port

Example:

YAML
kind: NetworkPolicy
ServiceEntry

Controls:

Plain Text
What external services exist?

Works at:

Plain Text
Service Mesh Layer
Layer 7

Example:

YAML
kind: ServiceEntry

Defines:

Plain Text
google.com
github.com
stripe.com
Comparison
NetworkPolicy	ServiceEntryKubernetes feature	Istio feature
L3/L4	L7
Controls traffic	Registers external services
IP/Port based	Hostname based
Doesn't define services	Defines external services
Interview Answer

NetworkPolicy controls connectivity between workloads using IPs and ports, whereas ServiceEntry registers external services with Istio so that traffic can be managed and observed.

4. Cilium NetworkPolicy vs Istio AuthorizationPolicy

Many engineers confuse these.

Cilium NetworkPolicy

Works at:

Plain Text
Network Layer

Controls:

Plain Text
Can packets flow?

Example:

Plain Text
app1 → app2:80 ✅
app1 → app2:443 ❌

Resource:

YAML
kind: CiliumNetworkPolicy
Istio AuthorizationPolicy

Works at:

Plain Text
Application Layer

Controls:

Plain Text
Who can call what API?

Example:

Plain Text
GET /orders ✅
DELETE /orders ❌

Resource:

YAML
kind: AuthorizationPolicy
Example

Cilium:

Plain Text
Allow TCP/80

Istio:

Plain Text
Allow GET
Deny DELETE
Interview Answer

Cilium NetworkPolicy controls packet-level communication using eBPF, while Istio AuthorizationPolicy controls request-level access such as users, JWTs, HTTP methods, and paths.

5. How Does mTLS STRICT Work?

Normal TLS:

Plain Text
Client verifies Server

mTLS:

Plain Text
Client verifies Server
Server verifies Client
 

Both workloads prove identity.

Istio mTLS Flow
Plain Text
App1
|
Envoy Sidecar
|
Certificate Exchange
|
Envoy Sidecar
|
App2

Certificates come from:

Plain Text
Istiod
STRICT Mode
YAML
mtls:
mode: STRICT

Behavior:

Plain Text
mTLS Traffic ✅
 
Plain Traffic ❌
Interview Answer

In STRICT mode, workloads must use mutual TLS. Any plaintext connection attempt is rejected because the sidecars require certificate-based authentication.

6. What Happens if a Workload Has No Sidecar?

Suppose:

Plain Text
app1 → has sidecar
app2 → no sidecar

STRICT mTLS enabled.

Flow:

Plain Text
app1
|
mTLS
|
app2

app2 cannot speak mTLS.

Result:

Plain Text
Connection Failure

Most commonly:

Plain Text
503
upstream connect error
Interview Answer

A workload without a sidecar cannot participate in mesh features such as mTLS, traffic management, or telemetry. Under STRICT mTLS, communication typically fails.

